DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       xxiimmppttooppppmm  reads  an Xim file as input and produces a PPM image as out‐
       put.  The Xim toolkit is included in the contrib tree  of  the  X.V11R4
       release.


OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In  addition  to  the options common to all programs based on libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), xxiimmttooppppmm  recognizes  the
       following command line option:

       You can abbreviate any option to its shortest unique prefix.



       ----aallpphhaaoouutt==_a_l_p_h_a_-_f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e
              xxiimmttooppppmm  creates a PGM file containing the transparency channel
              values in the input image.  If the input image doesn't contain a
              transparency  channel, the _a_l_p_h_a_-_f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e file contains all zero
              (transparent) transparency values.  If you don't  specify  ----aall‐‐
              pphhaaoouutt,  xxiimmttooppppmm  does not generate a transparency file, and if
              the input image has a transparency channel, xxiimmttooppppmm simply dis‐
              cards it.

              If  you specify -- as the filename, xxiimmttooppppmm writes the transpar‐
              ency output to Standard Output and discards the image.

              Actually, an Xim image can contain an arbitrary  fourth  channel
              -- it need not be a transparency channel.  xxiimmttooppppmm extracts any
              fourth channel it finds as described above; it doesn't matter if
              it is a transparency channel or not.

              See ppaammccoommpp(1) for one way to use the transparency output file.




